{
  "abstract": "An original result on Morrey's problem for integrands on real 2x2 matrices, proved in Lean 4: for every p > 0, an F that is isotropic (invariant under rotations on both sides), positively p-homogeneous and rank-one convex is quasiconvex (Theorem A). The proof for integrands that change sign integrates scaled truncated determinants over the radius (Proposition 6.8: for every convex N with N(0) <= 0, an explicit radial function of N is quasiconvex). What a sign condition adds, range by range:\n\nFor p >= 2 and F >= 0, F is polyconvex, hence quasiconvex, with a polyaffine support at every matrix in closed form (Theorems 1.1 and 1.2); this answers Question 1.7 of Guerra and Kristensen (Arch. Ration. Mech. Anal. 245, 2022), who proved it at the multiples of orthogonal matrices. For p > 2, F is polyconvex if and only if it is rank-one convex and nonnegative, and for p = 2 no sign condition is needed (Theorem 1.3). For 1 <= p < 2 and F >= 0, F has at every matrix a touching minorant that is a nonnegative combination of the Burkholder function and its mirror image (Theorem 1.5). Hence, for 1 < p < 2, F is quasiconvex (Corollary 1.6 (a)), by the quasiconvexity of the Burkholder function, which Proposition 6.8 gives for every p > 1 (Corollary 6.11). Proposition 6.8 rests on the quasiconvexity of the truncated determinant, claimed as Theorem 1 of the preprint of Agazzi et al., arXiv:2609.28311, and taken here from lean4-iwaniec, the Lean formalization with which that preprint was audited. For p = 1, F is convex, with no sign condition, a theorem of Dacorogna (Corollary 1.6 (b)). For 0 < p < 1, F is zero, with neither isotropy nor a sign condition (Proposition 1.7).\n\nThe paper is docs/HomogeneousIsotropic/paper/main.tex in the repository.",
